---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Who assigns ASNs?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "The five Regional Internet Registries (ARIN, RIPE NCC, APNIC, LACNIC, AFRINIC), which also allocate the IP blocks each AS announces." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Do I need an ASN?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Only if you operate a network that talks BGP with multiple providers. Hosting a server on someone else's network never requires one." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Why did an IP's ASN change?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Address blocks get transferred between networks, or announcements change during incidents; lookups reflect the current announcer." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Is an ASN public information?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Yes: ASN, name, and announced prefixes are public routing data, visible in every IP lookup." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "What is a private ASN?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Ranges reserved for internal BGP (not routed publicly); used inside large enterprise networks." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can one company have many ASNs?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Yes: after acquisitions or for separate product networks; each announces its own ranges." } }
  ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="What Is an ASN (Autonomous System Number)?"
  description="ASN explained: what autonomous systems are, how ASNs route internet traffic via BGP, and what an IP's ASN tells you in an IP lookup."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["IP & Networking", "/ip-and-networking/"], ["What Is an ASN"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ip-and-networking/" class="hover:text-accent">IP & Networking</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">What Is an ASN</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">What Is an ASN (Autonomous System Number)?</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">An Autonomous System is a network, or group of networks, operated under one administration with one routing policy: an ISP, a cloud provider, a university, a big company. Its ASN is the public ID it uses to announce its IP addresses to the rest of the internet.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">ASN essentials</h2>
    <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">What it is</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">One routing domain</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Example</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">AS15169 = Google</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">Why it matters</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Reputation and verification</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#how-asns-route-the-internet">How ASNs route the internet</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-an-asn-tells-you-in-practice">What an ASN tells you in practice</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="how-asns-route-the-internet">How ASNs route the internet</h2>
      <p>The internet has no central switch: it is ~75,000 autonomous systems exchanging reachability via BGP (Border Gateway Protocol). Each AS announces the IP ranges it owns to its neighbors; those announcements propagate, and every router builds a map of where to send traffic. When you run an IP lookup, the ASN in the result is the network currently announcing that address.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="what-an-asn-tells-you-in-practice">What an ASN tells you in practice</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li><strong class="text-ink">Who operates an address:</strong> AS13335 is Cloudflare, AS15169 is Google, AS16509 is AWS. In logs and IP lookups, the ASN is the fastest identity signal.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Reputation context:</strong> mail from a residential ASN behaves differently from mail from a hosting ASN; filters notice.</li><li><strong class="text-ink">Routing health:</strong> BGP hijacks are ASN-level events; monitoring your own ASN’s announcements is a real (if advanced) security practice.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Who assigns ASNs?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The five Regional Internet Registries (ARIN, RIPE NCC, APNIC, LACNIC, AFRINIC), which also allocate the IP blocks each AS announces.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need an ASN?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Only if you operate a network that talks BGP with multiple providers. Hosting a server on someone else's network never requires one.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why did an IP's ASN change?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Address blocks get transferred between networks, or announcements change during incidents; lookups reflect the current announcer.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is an ASN public information?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: ASN, name, and announced prefixes are public routing data, visible in every IP lookup.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is a private ASN?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Ranges reserved for internal BGP (not routed publicly); used inside large enterprise networks.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can one company have many ASNs?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: after acquisitions or for separate product networks; each announces its own ranges.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/ip-lookup/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">IP lookup tool →</a></li><li><a href="/public-vs-private-ip/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Reserved IP ranges →</a></li><li><a href="/ip-lookup/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">IP and networking hub →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
